WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.640
A factor lands on the outside of a neuron.

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.340
How does that switch on a signal inside?

3
00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:10.653
Start with the receptor.

4
00:00:11.593 --> 00:00:13.913
A Trk receptor sits in the neuron's membrane.

5
00:00:15.017 --> 00:00:16.857
Outside, it binds the factor.

6
00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:20.097
Inside, it carries a tyrosine kinase domain.

7
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:26.470
Now the factor. Each neurotrophin is a dimer, so it has two binding faces.

8
00:00:27.583 --> 00:00:29.103
A dimer has two faces.

9
00:00:30.083 --> 00:00:31.823
What can it do to two receptors at once?

10
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:41.567
It can hold two Trk receptors at once, and bring them together.

11
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:44.437
A crystal structure shows this.

12
00:00:45.357 --> 00:00:49.257
One NGF dimer holds two TrkA domains, one on each side.

13
00:00:50.217 --> 00:00:55.157
Their kinase domains, now side by side, phosphorylate each other on tyrosines.

14
00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:01.040
The phosphorylated tyrosines become docking sites for proteins inside the cell.

15
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.800
They start two survival branches you have met before.

16
00:01:05.883 --> 00:01:07.923
One runs from Ras to ERK.

17
00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:11.477
The other runs from PI3K to Akt.

18
00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:13.990
Now back to the first puzzle.

19
00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.930
How does a neuron know which factor is its own?

20
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.140
Each neurotrophin prefers particular Trk receptors.

21
00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:27.633
NGF prefers TrkA. BDNF and NT-4 prefer TrkB.

22
00:01:28.493 --> 00:01:32.953
NT-3 prefers TrkC, and activates TrkA and TrkB more weakly.

23
00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:39.187
The selective differences lie in a few contact surfaces, such as the factor's amino

24
00:01:39.187 --> 00:01:40.187
terminus.

25
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.910
The overall plan is the same in another pair, NT-4 with TrkB.

26
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.360
So the answer is this.

27
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.060
A neuron answers the factor whose Trk receptor it makes.

28
00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:55.090
A neuron that makes TrkA is switched on by NGF.

29
00:01:56.183 --> 00:01:59.243
One that makes TrkC is switched on by NT-3.

30
00:02:00.233 --> 00:02:02.793
The factor does not know which neuron it is for.

31
00:02:03.613 --> 00:02:04.753
The receptor sorts it.

32
00:02:05.633 --> 00:02:11.093
And p75? It binds NGF at a site different from where TrkA binds.

33
00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:15.207
What it does with that binding is the rest of this lesson.

34
00:02:16.217 --> 00:02:21.057
The takeaway: the factor pairs two receptors, and the receptor a neuron makes

35
00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:22.577
decides which factor it hears.
